/* Whether a dance was written down — and whether the paper is enough to bring
 * it back, which is a different question and the one worth asking.
 *
 * This atlas files a work's survival as `notated` among its statuses, and
 * that word sounds settled. The Dance Notation Bureau, which holds the scores,
 * says otherwise one score at a time: 4 of the 87 scores here can be
 * performed from without asking anyone. 10 works have nothing but a score the
 * Bureau itself calls incomplete and not suitable for staging. The others need
 * the Bureau's permission, or the estate's, or a coach in the room.
 *
 *   notator  who wrote it down, and `notated` the year they did
 *   level    how hard the score is to read, in the Bureau's own grades
 *   status   handwritten, typed, rough, incomplete — the physical object
 *   pages    its length, which is the only measure of a dance nobody argues with
 *   perm     yes | no | ask — whether it may be performed from
 *   says     the Bureau's own words about what the score can be used for
 *   about    the programme note the Bureau reproduces, where the record has
 *            one — the work described by the people who made it
 *   url      the catalogue entry
 *
 * Generated from research/dance-notation by build-data.cjs. Do not edit.
 */
